Regarding the phasing out of subsidies, to avoid a situation where companies become too reliant on them, policymakers can implement a gradual phase-out plan. This plan should be clearly communicated to the affected companies well in advance, allowing them time to adjust their business models and invest in sustainable technologies. Additionally, subsidies can be designed with built-in incentives for innovation and efficiency, such as requiring companies to match subsidy funds with their own investments in research and development or in the adoption of cleaner technologies.

It's also important to monitor the impact of subsidies closely and adjust the phase-out plan as necessary. This might involve regular assessments of the competitiveness of subsidized companies, their investment in sustainable technologies, and their overall environmental performance. By taking a gradual and transparent approach to phasing out subsidies, and by ensuring that the process is free from conflicts of interest, policymakers can help companies transition towards sustainability without creating undue dependence on government support.

